      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To determine the incidence and nature of adverse events associated with the induction of rush Hymenoptera venom immunotherapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective descriptive case study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "The asthma and allergy unit at a major metropolitan teaching hospital, between 1 January 1989 and 30 June 1999."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "All patients with anaphylaxis to stings of Hymenoptera insects who received rush venom immunotherapy as inpatients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Hypersensitivity reactions to venom administration, including angioedema, skin rashes, hypotension and asthma, as well as any other adverse events related to the inpatient stay."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "68 venom-allergic patients received 73 courses of rush immunotherapy; 89% were desensitised to honey bee venom, 10% to yellow jacket wasp venom, and one to paper wasp venom. Hypersensitivity reactions occurred after 36 subcutaneous injections (3.8% of all injections given) in 26 patients (38%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In our cohort, immunotherapy was accompanied by a high incidence of adverse systemic events during the induction phase. Immunotherapy should only be given by experienced staff in centres where there are facilities for resuscitation."
        }
      ]},
